#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "48364248-d296-502c-96da-d1905be35627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12707" :question/problem "Which statement is logically equivalent to the following conditional statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ If you take an umbrella, then you will stay dry." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ You take an umbrella.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will stay dry.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You do not take an umbrella.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ You do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ Either you do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "caa49bb8-c66f-56cb-a3d2-a7616abee38c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12708" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor \\neg B.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv \\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nNow, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n\\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv ( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(A \\land B) \\lor A \\equiv A.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a65dc8-528a-5c3c-b518-a2bbecc551c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12709" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y \\equiv \\neg (X \\land Y)$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv \\neg X \\land Y$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv X \\land Y$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\neg X \\lor \\neg Y,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $\\neg (X\\land Y).$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nX \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\equiv \\neg (X\\land Y)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right) \\equiv \\neg \\left(\\neg (X\\land Y)\\right).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX\\land Y.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)$ is equivalent to $X \\land Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/id #uuid "54512495-0ca8-55a3-834d-0b70f96a5fe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13231" :question/problem "Which conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement below?\n\n$\\qquad$ Use sunscreen, or you will get sunburn." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ Use sunscreen.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will get sunburn.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\lor B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\Rightarrow B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You don't use sunscreen.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ If you don't use sunscreen, then you will get sunburn."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411000} {:answer/id #uuid "85a8fd88-3b39-5857-b51b-e87763378f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411023} {:answer/id #uuid "a9820fe9-88d1-532e-b488-c19e1e9c8373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411031} {:answer/id #uuid "da347817-d713-5445-9cf9-fd508c9e189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411041} {:answer/id #uuid "e0566476-5f2e-585a-a6da-7238085c09d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d46aa30-73c8-590c-a750-bc1fe65993d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cc8fa9c-919a-5715-b893-d15125898b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-102732" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬P⇒Q).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(¬(¬P)∨Q).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬P)∨Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598a18e6-a7e1-5198-85c8-cd85ff5e8356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386043} {:answer/id #uuid "911f6309-746b-5ff8-84bb-2e4ba995dfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386044} {:answer/id #uuid "363eeec8-972d-506f-9054-9bb9d2d6a13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386045} {:answer/id #uuid "abda69df-8ef3-5465-b050-742379020027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386046} {:answer/id #uuid "de236ff7-8d36-54e6-bce4-c965e0d96754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "911f6309-746b-5ff8-84bb-2e4ba995dfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386044} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8c4ca2-b4ee-5544-b0ba-f01f0093c925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a2aac01-1463-56cc-bce5-1c99c13e924c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102736" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬P⇒(P∧Q).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬P⇒(P∧Q)≡¬(¬P)∨(P∧Q).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬P)∨(P∧Q)≡P∨(P∧Q).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7ae9dc-bc1c-5952-86bf-ac64d05dcdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬Q∧¬R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378720} {:answer/id #uuid "27a7f796-45fe-50c0-b137-2daefb80bacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬R∧Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378721} {:answer/id #uuid "9936400b-0feb-592e-a451-6d2689e03c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬Q∨¬R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378722} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ed7cca-09a4-5119-9585-89d6dbbd28dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378723} {:answer/id #uuid "17494fe9-de23-5826-8357-b89b12c9e401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ed7cca-09a4-5119-9585-89d6dbbd28dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378723} :answer-field/id #uuid "84fa8547-8670-5dc3-8bc0-1884fffcd80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "975bf3d1-7639-5d28-a9e4-e095a51c29d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-1470" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $Q⇒(¬Q∧R).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\nQ⇒(¬Q∧R)≡¬Q∨(¬Q∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n¬Q∨(¬Q∧R)≡¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬Q.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c85ca4cc-8f80-55a2-aa3d-0627430c57eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬R∧S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378734} {:answer/id #uuid "2e591199-c323-52e9-b477-e699c7824c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬R∧¬S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378735} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0dafe5-511c-5104-969d-4a292ce8837b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S∨¬R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378736} {:answer/id #uuid "4002dcb5-d1a5-53c5-b20e-c2a9722bfe18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "S∨R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378737} {:answer/id #uuid "05af80e5-2316-5d6b-94b1-db808e739ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R∨¬S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e591199-c323-52e9-b477-e699c7824c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬R∧¬S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378735} :answer-field/id #uuid "239bd4b6-c671-5ca0-9b41-523ee50c2d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f073aaca-ab2b-5cf0-b60f-e52fb1e200c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-1478" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬R⇒S).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬R⇒S)≡¬(¬(¬R)∨S).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬R)∨S)≡¬(R∨S).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(R∨S)≡¬R∧¬S\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬R∧¬S.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab520b5-59f6-5feb-953d-189ae99c37f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Study, and you won't pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411018} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcbcf84-c1f5-5b0f-adcd-61e18ef3dcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186