this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Study, or you won't pass the exam."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b8bb54-cf99-5e6c-b24b-e2230fac2b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Eat your vegetables, and you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379149} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca58dd3-81a2-5e75-bf06-3b71fe5517d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you eat your vegetables, or you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379150} {:answer/id #uuid "c603d67b-0a2a-57b8-a62f-31a8b835af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379151} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb9d333-1346-5b47-a1f0-9297d9005f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't eat your vegetables, or you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379152} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e7f4d1-92b5-5b76-9a70-b6dda138dcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c603d67b-0a2a-57b8-a62f-31a8b835af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379151} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bf1056-ffc3-5ba7-bc23-3b671a26a339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e854e89-958c-5853-be27-7985def8fc91" :question/math-academy-id "q-182458" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you eat your vegetables, then you can have dessert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You eat your vegetables.\n\n- $B:$ You can have dessert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't eat your vegetables.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef745713-2d35-5b7c-8ffa-849019bc9a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379162} {:answer/id #uuid "09ee9ee7-3301-505d-b0b4-fa2550135d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379163} {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e643c-4c88-5ee5-a9cb-bc94557a292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379165} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f93cbd-83f5-5a7a-b05a-db016463f018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f9c689-8577-52b8-aa74-632d353fd931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "866aa1bc-982d-58ab-8e07-dd5148fa5235" :question/math-academy-id "q-182470" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54794834-147f-5ca2-b3aa-a53057dc42b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∨N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411012} {:answer/id #uuid "67bc61ce-a9ad-55ef-865b-df1dad087605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬M∨N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411016} {:answer/id #uuid "912c3b4c-072c-58f6-b64b-c9f5e02a6ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∧N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411026} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5b11e-6587-5ea7-9a34-acdcb5a0d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∧¬N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411037} {:answer/id #uuid "c15a47c8-499b-599d-8986-58576b3db58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∧N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912c3b4c-072c-58f6-b64b-c9f5e02a6ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∧N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411026} :answer-field/id #uuid "863e1035-b7f5-5d5b-b991-ac77fd6a7db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17e8ba9f-418a-585c-9332-bdf5c1ee589a" :question/math-academy-id "q-182493" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $M⇒¬N.$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nM⇒¬N≡¬M∨¬N\n$$\n\nUsing De Morgan's law, we reach\n\n$$\n¬M∨¬N≡¬(M∧N).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬(M∧N).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411013} {:answer/id #uuid "7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411019} {:answer/id #uuid "9233ceaf-9f1c-5208-b9cd-674ecda2cce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411027} {:answer/id #uuid "b621cd70-8e5a-56ee-b272-20c6098bad66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411035} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b04d6d-0c18-5c36-91f6-4f598bc216d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411013} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb0a268b-ca46-5b91-9359-0e418e71bab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-182494" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(X⇒Y).$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(X⇒Y)≡¬(¬X∨Y)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨Y)≡¬(¬X)∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "636ebc41-322a-50d9-8335-010fc61f221b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411014} {:answer/id #uuid "79ead6e4-4892-5762-bdb7-b1413f5768d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411017} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0752bb-bd77-5627-a153-d3ceecd28fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411020} {:answer/id #uuid "a98771cb-023d-5e3f-bb77-fb7e1b8058aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411032} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d164c1-cdee-5643-a16e-3824d4c07a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0752bb-bd77-5627-a153-d3ceecd28fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411020} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ff40c2-b221-5f65-9fd3-02b3e136bbd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7ec246a-1c3b-5830-ae08-ed99cbcc20da" :question/math-academy-id "q-182503" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒B≡¬A∨B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∨¬B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∧¬B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6f81fa-ef3e-50ce-8cd2-a95e102cf1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409575} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebe90ec-2497-5de0-b5bd-ba3d0c79d56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409576} {:answer/id #uuid "cff472a8-e087-557d-80e6-5d535af2d5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409578} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca4c9-2b1c-5f16-824c-d7eea7cfd316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} :answer-field/id #uuid "7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031baea5-6ff9-513e-b2ec-02a90e898fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410998} {:answer/id #uuid "087177ce-72c4-5ef2-938f-b10e9e8b40fc" 