f $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not follow the recipe.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151b8ca4-58d5-5c25-9aaa-e1dc70bfd781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wake up early, or you won't have time for breakfast." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411004} {:answer/id #uuid "24af826b-425f-56bf-9262-da3d54c093a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't wake up early, and you won't have time for breakfast." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411006} {:answer/id #uuid "47287ee3-35fd-5008-b4df-10ce8e6df1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wake up early, and you will have time for breakfast." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411008} {:answer/id #uuid "b6671899-a182-531f-b237-54941551781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wake up early, and you won't have time for breakfast." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411036} {:answer/id #uuid "f62e14c2-d216-5de5-99af-9a4d099a2b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.