uivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You buy a ticket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f48d79-1191-5d91-bccd-c73e834a470c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't win this game, then you will make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410999} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce79388-e687-51e4-8c79-52bb8958a2df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411002} {:answer/id #uuid "33a5b76d-0c32-5808-9831-19236d7bd6e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Win this game, and you will make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411007} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae411bb-3f11-5442-8eba-d9d404fe68e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411025} {:answer/id #uuid "d52f382a-e459-5416-8be7-d740d5696d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you win this game, then you will make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ae411bb-3f11-5442-8eba-d9d404fe68e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411025} :answer-field/id #uuid "234605e9-9e3b-5aee-bd89-a1493234c17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "283e6f42-c6ed-5da0-9085-3d8a16902757" :question/math-academy-id "q-182589" :question/problem "$\\,$ Win this game, or you won't make it to the playoffs.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Win this game.\n\n- $B:$ You won’t make it to the playoffs.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't win this game.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d443744-cec7-50fa-b09c-8602760601c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379262} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2fe104-86f6-5a71-b4ce-e3438ccc1c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you will understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379263} {:answer/id #uuid "805f4b30-a877-5ba5-9626-70177aa117fd" 